<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Faisal ibn Turki Al Saud</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1865">1865</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-saud">House of Saud</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Faisal ibn Turki <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-saud">Al Saud</a> was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> of the Second Saudi State, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Nejd</a>, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a> in two reigns, 1834–1838 and 1843–1865. Between them an Egyptian expedition took him captive to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, but he escaped and regained power. He backed his ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdullah-ibn-rashid">Abdullah ibn Rashid</a> in taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hail">Ha'il</a> in 1836, founding the Rashidi Emirate of Jabal Shammar, and his long second reign consolidated the emirate.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Faisal was born in 1785, the son of Imam Turki bin Abdullah. He was among the members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-saud">Al Saud</a> taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> after Ibrahim Pasha, son of Muhammad Ali, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diriyah">Diriyah</a> in May 1819, and he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a> in 1827-1828. In 1830 he was sent on campaign to Al Hasa in the east. While he was away, his father was assassinated in 1834 by Mishari bin Abdul Rahman, a second cousin. Faisal hurried back, his troops stormed the castle and killed Mishari, and the town pledged allegiance to him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His refusal to pay tribute to the Egyptian forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> brought an expedition into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a>. The Egyptian governor Khurshid Pasha backed a rival from the senior line of the family, Khalid bin Saud, and Faisal fled to the princes of the Bani Tamim. In December 1838 he tried to come to terms with Khurshid Pasha but was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> with his brother Jiluwi, his sons Abdullah and Muhammad and a cousin. Released in 1843, after the Egyptian troops had left Najd, he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a> and easily defeated his third cousin Abdullah bin Thunayan, who had seized power from Khalid.</p><p>In his second reign he accepted Ottoman supremacy and was named governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a>. He subdued opposing tribes and took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">Al-Ahsa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatif">Qatif</a> and Saihat, whose revenues, levied as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakat">zakat</a>, funded his treasury. His attempts on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> failed, partly because of British opposition to Saudi expansion in the Gulf. In June 1851 Qatari forces under Mohammed bin Thani and his son Jassim bin Mohammed defeated his army in the Battle of Mesaimeer, and an agreement of 25 July 1851 gave him an annual payment from Ali bin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalifa">Khalifa</a> of Bahrain in return for leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatar">Qatar</a> alone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the 1850s and 1860s his moves against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trucial-states">Trucial States</a> were halted by British forces. Faisal drew his income from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakat">zakat</a>, import duties, pilgrim fees, a fifth of the spoils of raids, fines, his own domains and tribute from Bahrain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a>. In his last years he grew frail and blind, and his heir Abdullah was the effective ruler. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a> in December 1865 after a long illness. His four sons, Abdullah, Saud, Muhammad and Abdul Rahman, born of two mothers, fell into the infighting that eventually destroyed the state. One of his daughters married Abdullah bin Rashid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hail">Ha'il</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Faisal bin Turki Al Saud (1785–1865)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1836 Abdullah ibn Rashid took control of Ha'il, the main town of the Jabal Shammar mountains, with the backing of the Saudi ruler <b>Faisal ibn Turki</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-jabal-shammar">Emirate of Jabal Shammar</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Faisal ibn Turki</b>, who ruled twice, consolidated it; between his reigns Egypt occupied Riyadh again from 1838 to 1843.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-nejd">Emirate of Nejd</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=23,46&amp;year=1855" aria-label="Arabian Peninsula on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Faisal ibn Turki Al Saud"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3909 5530h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3909 5530h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=23,46&amp;year=1855">What was here in 1855? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
